sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Among the possible arrangements of a small group of cells, the most stable (and therefore commonest) are those in which the cell-to-cell contacts are most evenly divided among the cells, and the least stable are those in which one cell has a predominant number of contacts.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Most stable cell groupings spread contacts evenly; unstable ones concentrate them";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Stability of eight-cell arrangements”. Scope: small aggregates of cells or drops (segmenting eggs, oil-drop models)"
}
